let __v_966 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"psychology\",\"*Thinking, Fast and Slow* by Daniel Kahneman is the one that changes how you see your own decisions, though it is a long read; *The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat* by Oliver Sacks is the shorter, stranger way in.\"],[\"philosophy\",\"*Think* by Simon Blackburn is written for someone who has never read any: a chapter a question, no jargon. *Sophie's World* by Jostein Gaarder tells the whole history of it as a novel if you want the story instead.\"],[\"economics\",\"*The Undercover Economist* by Tim Harford explains prices, markets and why things cost what they do without a single equation. *The Armchair Economist* by Steven Landsburg is the more argumentative one.\"],[\"science\",\"*A Short History of Nearly Everything* by Bill Bryson is the one to start with: how we know what we know, and funny throughout. *The Selfish Gene* by Richard Dawkins if it is evolution you want.\"],[\"climate\",\"*How to Avoid a Climate Disaster* by Bill Gates is the plainest account of where the emissions actually come from and what would fix each one, without the gloom. *The Uninhabitable Earth* by David Wallace-Wells is the bleak one.\"],[\"maths\",\"*Alex's Adventures in Numberland* by Alex Bellos is maths as stories about people and puzzles, so nothing feels like homework. *How Not to Be Wrong* by Jordan Ellenberg is the one about using it to think.\"],[\"politics\",\"*How Westminster Works... and Why It Doesn't* by Ian Dunt is the short angry guide to how British government actually runs. *The Prince* by Machiavelli if you want the old one people still quote.\"],[\"poetry\",\"*Staying Alive* edited by Neil Astley is the anthology to start with: modern poems in English, sorted by what they are about rather than by poet. Read one a night and skip anything that does not land.\"],[\"language\",\"*The Language Instinct* by Steven Pinker is the readable one on how language works in the head, and *Mother Tongue* by Bill Bryson is the fun history of English if that is the half you want.\"],[\"gardening\",\"*How to Garden* (DK) is the beginner's standard: a photograph for every job, month by month, and no assumed knowledge. *The Allotment Book* by Andi Clevely if it is a plot rather than a border.\"],[\"chess\",\"*Bobby Fischer Teaches Chess* is all positions and no theory, so you learn by doing, and free games online afterwards make it stick. *Chess for Dummies* if you want the openings named as well.\"],[\"knitting\",\"*Stitch 'n Bitch* by Debbie Stoller: cast on, knit, purl, then patterns you can actually finish. A pair of 4mm needles and a ball of aran wool is the whole kit and it costs about ten pounds.\"],[\"crochet\",\"*The Happy Hooker* by Debbie Stoller is the crochet one from the same series: the stitches step by step, then small things worth making. One 4mm hook and a ball of aran wool to start.\"],[\"sewing\",\"*The Sewing Book* by Alison Smith (DK) photographs every technique from a hem to a zip, so it stays useful for years. Practise on an old pillowcase rather than good fabric.\"],[\"drawing\",\"*Drawing on the Right Side of the Brain* by Betty Edwards, because beginners draw what they think is there instead of what is, which is the thing the exercises fix. A 2B pencil and cheap paper is the kit.\"],[\"painting\",\"*Watercolour for the Absolute Beginner* by Matthew Palmer: three washes and you have painted something, with nothing assumed. Cold-pressed 300gsm paper decides more than the paints do, and acrylics are the easier way in.\"],[\"photography\",\"*Understanding Exposure* by Bryan Peterson, since exposure is what separates a snapshot from a photograph and the book works on any camera, phone included.\"],[\"guitar\",\"*Hal Leonard Guitar Method, Book 1*: chords and songs from the first pages, with the audio included. Any cheap acoustic plays well enough to learn on.\"],[\"piano\",\"*Alfred's Basic Adult Piano Course, Book 1* starts at the very beginning and has both hands together within a few pages. A 61-key keyboard is enough to learn on.\"],[\"baking\",\"*Flour Water Salt Yeast* by Ken Forkish for bread: four ingredients, a schedule, and a loaf that tastes like a bakery's. *The Great British Bake Off: How to Bake* if it is cakes and pastry you want.\"],[\"cooking\",\"*Salt, Fat, Acid, Heat* by Samin Nosrat teaches why a dish works, so you stop following recipes. *Delia's Complete Cookery Course* if you would rather be told exactly what to do.\"],[\"running\",\"*Run Walk Run* by Jeff Galloway: walking breaks get a beginner to 5k without the injuries that stop people. Get shoes fitted at a running shop rather than bought from a supermarket.\"],[\"yoga\",\"*Light on Yoga* by B.K.S. Iyengar is the classic reference, but go to two or three classes first, because a book cannot see your back.\"],[\"dog training\",\"*Perfect Puppy* by Gwen Bailey covers the first weeks, house training and the five things to teach first. *The Dog Listener* by Jan Fennell if the dog is out of puppyhood already.\"],[\"birdwatching\",\"*The RSPB Pocket Guide to British Birds* names what you are looking at, and a pair of 8x42 binoculars is the one purchase: second hand is fine, and the garden birds are the place to start.\"],[\"astronomy\",\"*Turn Left at Orion* by Guy Consolmagno: what to look for first from a back garden, and where it is. Binoculars find most of it, so no telescope yet.\"],[\"coding\",\"*Python Crash Course* by Eric Matthes, which has you build a game and a small web app instead of reading about variables. Free tutorials afterwards are fine.\"],[\"investing\",\"*Smarter Investing* by Tim Hale: the boring index-fund argument, with the numbers and the costs. Nothing else is needed until that makes sense.\"],[\"money\",\"*Money: A User's Guide* by Laura Whateley takes bills, debt, pensions and savings in the order they matter for somebody in the UK.\"],[\"history\",\"*A Little History of the World* by E. H. Gombrich: short chapters, written for a child but never childish, and it gives you the map everything else hangs on.\"],[\"wine\",\"*Wine Folly* by Madeline Puckette shows the grapes and regions as pictures, which is how they stick, and the tasting words come after.\"],[\"woodworking\",\"*Woodworking Basics* by Peter Korn takes one joint at a time with hand tools, which is the right order. A hardpoint saw, a chisel, a square and a bench hook do more than a garage of machines.\"],[\"fishing\",\"*Fishing for Dummies* covers the rig, the bait and the knots for coarse fishing, and a day ticket at a club water is the way to try it. Rod licence first if you are in England or Wales.\"],[\"climbing\",\"*How to Rock Climb!* by John Long is the standard for the basics, ropes and knots. Go to an indoor wall with hired shoes and try bouldering first, since it needs no partner.\"],[\"diy\",\"*The Reader's Digest Complete DIY Manual* covers the jobs in a house from a shelf to a radiator, and a Haynes manual for your own model if it is the car.\"],[\"car\",\"A Haynes manual for your exact model, since the engine has to match, plus a socket set. That covers servicing, brakes and most of an MOT.\"]]}")?;
