let __v_6300 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"chord\",\"A chord is three or more notes played together: C major is C, E and G, all at once. Major chords sound settled and minor ones sound sad, which is most of why a song has a mood at all.\"],[\"key\",\"The key is the home note a piece is built around and comes back to: a song in C keeps landing on C. The notes it uses are that key's scale, and a key change lifts the whole song.\"],[\"scale\",\"A scale is the ladder of notes a key is made of, up or down: do re mi fa sol la ti do. A major scale sounds bright and a minor one darker, and both are eight notes with the first repeated at the top.\"],[\"octave\",\"An octave is the gap between a note and the same note higher or lower, eight notes up the scale. It sounds like the same note because the frequency exactly doubles: A is 440 vibrations a second, the A above it 880.\"],[\"time signature\",\"The two numbers at the start of the music. The top one is how many beats are in a bar and the bottom one which note gets the beat, so 4/4 is four quarter notes to the bar (most pop) and 3/4 is a waltz.\"],[\"bar\",\"A bar is one group of beats, marked off by the bar lines on the page: in 4/4 that is four beats to a bar, which is why eight bars is the length of most pop verses. A bar and a measure are the same thing.\"],[\"beat\",\"The beat is the steady pulse you clap or tap along to; the rhythm is the pattern played on top of it. Four beats to the bar is most pop, three is a waltz, and a drummer keeps the beat while everyone else plays around it.\"],[\"tempo\",\"Tempo is how fast the music goes, counted in beats per minute: 60 is one a second and a ballad, 120 is two a second and a dance track. A metronome clicks it for you.\"],[\"bpm\",\"BPM is beats per minute, how fast the music goes: 60 is one a second and a ballad, 120 is two a second and a dance track. A metronome clicks it for you.\"],[\"pitch\",\"Pitch is how high or low a note is, set by how fast the sound vibrates: a short string or a tight one vibrates faster and sounds higher.\"],[\"melody\",\"The melody is the tune - the line of single notes you could sing on its own. The harmony is the chords underneath it, and the rhythm is the pattern in time.\"],[\"harmony\",\"Harmony is two or more notes sounding together, usually as the chords under the melody. It is what makes a bare tune sound full, and what a second singer adds.\"],[\"rhythm\",\"Rhythm is the pattern of the music in time - where the beats fall and which are strong. A drummer keeps it, and you can clap it without knowing any notes.\"],[\"pre chorus\",\"The pre-chorus is the short section between the verse and the chorus that builds towards it: the words change each time, the music lifts, and then the chorus lands. It is the ramp rather than the hook.\"],[\"chorus\",\"The chorus is the part that comes round again with the same words - the bit you can sing back after one listen, and usually the title. The verses in between change each time and carry the story, and a bridge is the one different section near the end.\"],[\"hook\",\"The hook is the short phrase that catches you: a chorus line, a riff or a bit of vocal you find yourself humming. A producer will often make it the first thing you hear in the song.\"],[\"riff\",\"A riff is a short repeated phrase of notes, usually on guitar or keys, that a song is built on: the opening of *Smoke on the Water* or *Seven Nation Army*. It is a hook you can play rather than sing.\"],[\"sample\",\"A sample is a piece of an existing recording lifted into a new track - a drum break, a vocal line or a chord. Hip hop and dance music are built on them, and the original artist is usually credited and paid.\"],[\"progression\",\"A chord progression is the order the chords come in, usually a short loop that repeats: C, G, A minor, F is the one behind a thousand pop songs. The loop is what the melody and the words sit on, and changing one chord changes the whole mood.\"],[\"key signature\",\"The key signature is the sharps or flats written at the start of every line of music, and they say which key the piece is in: one sharp is G major, one flat is F major, none is C major. It saves writing the same sharp or flat beside every note.\"],[\"arpeggio\",\"An arpeggio is a chord played one note at a time instead of all together: C, E, G, one after another, low to high. It is what fingerpicking is made of and what you hear when a piano player rolls through the chords.\"],[\"tab\",\"Tab (tablature) is guitar music written as six lines for the six strings, with a number on a line meaning that fret on that string: a 3 on the top line is the third fret of the top string. It is read left to right like a sentence, it skips the rhythm, and it is how most people learn a song by ear-free copying.\"],[\"interval\",\"An interval is the distance between two notes, counted in the steps of the scale: C up to G is a fifth, C up to E a third, and the same note twice an octave. Every chord and tune is made of them, so learning their sounds by ear is the single most useful bit of theory there is.\"],[\"clef\",\"A clef is the sign at the start of the staff that says which lines are which notes. Treble clef curls round the second line up, which is G, and is used for the right hand of a piano, the violin and most singing; bass clef covers the low lines for the left hand, the cello and the bass.\"],[\"middle c\",\"Middle C is the C nearest the middle of a piano keyboard, the one four white keys in from the left of the group of two black keys in the middle. Written in treble clef it sits on its own short line just below the staff, and in bass clef on a short line just above it, which is why it is the note that joins the two hands.\"],[\"transpose\",\"To transpose is to move a whole piece into a different key, keeping the tune and the shapes of the chords the same and shifting every note by the same amount - usually so a singer can reach it. On a guitar a capo transposes without changing anything you play; a keyboard has a transpose button that does it for you.\"],[\"metronome\",\"A metronome clicks a steady beat at whatever speed you set, in beats per minute, so you can hear whether you are rushing. Practise the hard bar at a speed you can play it clean three times running, then nudge it up five at a time - it is the cheapest way to get faster, and a phone app does the job.\"],[\"producer\",\"The producer is the person who shapes the recording: the arrangement, the sounds, how the parts fit, and the mix at the end. They are not the songwriter and not the engineer. In film the word means the person who runs the money and the schedule, a different job.\"]]}")?;
